Do everything `treerig up` does (register the worktree, start the shared router
and any shared services, start the services, run its hooks), then follow the
logs of the worktree's services until you press Ctrl-C. The services keep
running afterwards; `treerig down` stops them, and the session may stay open,
idle, until you press Ctrl-C. While a session is attached, `treerig worktree
remove` and a second `treerig dev` refuse, but `up`, `down` and `run` work as
usual. Ctrl-C, SIGTERM and SIGHUP end the session, stop the log follower and
release the lock, with exit status 130, 143 and 129. If `up` fails, so does
`dev`, the same way, and no logs are followed. If the log follower ends on its
own, because Docker went away or every container is gone, `dev` says so and
exits non-zero.

Usage:
  treerig dev [flags]

Flags:
      --config string   path to treerig.toml (default: nearest above cwd)
  -h, --help            help for dev

Global Flags:
      --progress mode   how to show progress: auto (redraw on a terminal) or
                        plain (one line per change) (default "auto")
  -v, --verbose         show what treerig runs, and every detail
